import GroupApproximation.GGT.HullSCRotatingFamily
import GroupApproximation.GGT.WPDAcylindricalHyperbolicity

/-!
# Hull's small cancellation theorem in acylindrically hyperbolic groups

M. Hull, *Small cancellation in acylindrically hyperbolic groups*,
Groups Geom. Dyn. 10 (2016) 1077-1119, Theorem 7.1, and the reduction of the
whole of Hull's Theorem 7.1 to its one-relator case.

`Manuscript.NonMF.HullFillCorrectedInputs.HullBallFormStatementNG` is the
statement this module discharges down to a single citation.  It is Hull's
theorem in the published injectivity-radius form, with the datum -- the
manuscript's separate remark on Hull's construction -- that `ker q` is normally
generated by `m` elements:

> Let `G` be acylindrically hyperbolic with Hull's alphabet `A`, let `N ≤ G` be
> suitable, let `t₁, …, t_m ∈ G` and let `R` be a radius.  Then there is a
> surjection `q : G ↠ Q` with `Q` acylindrically hyperbolic, `q` injective on
> the `R`-ball of `Γ(G,A)`, `q(tᵢ) ∈ q(N)` for every `i`, every element of
> finite order in `Q` the image of one of the same order in `G`, and `ker q`
> the normal closure of at most `m` elements.

## The mathematics, in dependency order

This is the chain of theorems a formal proof of Hull's Theorem 7.1 needs.  Each
entry names the source, the statement in the vocabulary of this repository, and
whether it is formalized here.

**(0) The space.**  `Γ(G,A)` is
`Manuscript.NonMF.AcylindricallyHyperbolic.Cayley`: the vertex set `G` with the
word metric of `A` and the translation action.  Four-point hyperbolicity is
`HullGeometry.IsHyperbolicSpace`, Osin's acylindricity is
`HullGeometry.IsAcylindrical`, loxodromy and independence are
`HullGeometry.IsLoxodromic` and `HullGeometry.Independent`, non-elementarity --
*contains two independent loxodromics* -- is
`HullGeometry.ActsNonElementarily`, and Hull's Definition 1.4 is
`Manuscript.NonMF.TorsionFree.Suitable`.  All formalized, in
`Sofic/HullSuitabilityGeometry.lean`.

**(1) Hyperbolically embedded subgroups** (Osin, *Acylindrically hyperbolic
groups*, Def. 4.25; DGO §4).  `{Hλ} ↪_h (G, X)` when `X ∪ ⋃ Hλ` generates `G`,
the coned-off graph `Γ(G, X ⊔ ⊔ Hλ)` is hyperbolic, and each `Hλ` carries a
locally finite relative metric.  The definition is
`GGT.RelGenSet.IsHyperbolicallyEmbedded` -- with the family primary and the
single-subgroup wrappers `GGT.IsHypEmbeddedOf`, `GGT.IsHypEmbedded` -- of
`GGT/WPDHyperbolicallyEmbedded.lean`, and it is used, not restated.
`HullSC.coneOff` of `GGT/HullSCConeOff.lean` is that relative generating set
for a single subgroup, read as a cone-off: adjoining every element of `H` as a
letter is exactly the cone of radius one over each coset,
`HullSC.coneOff_dist_le` proves the crushing, and
`HullSC.isHypEmbeddedOf_coneOff` proves that the cone-off is the witness for
`H ↪_h (G,A)`.

**(2) Hull's Theorem 3.12** (after Osin, Thm 1.2 and DGO Thm 4.42).  An
acylindrically hyperbolic `G` has a generating set `A`, in general infinite,
with `Γ(G,A)` hyperbolic, the translation action acylindrical, and `G`
non-elementary; and any hyperbolically embedded family can be arranged inside
it.  The first half is
`Manuscript.NonMF.TorsionFree.exists_hullGeneratingSet`, which is a *proof*
rather than a citation because acylindrical hyperbolicity is taken in the
Cayley-graph form; the second half is what makes step (3) available.

**(3) Elementary closures** (DGO Thm 6.8).  A loxodromic element `g` of an
acylindrical action is WPD, its elementary closure
`E(g) = {h : ∃ n > 0, h gⁿ h⁻¹ = g^{±n}}` is the unique maximal virtually
cyclic subgroup containing `g`, and `E(g) ↪_h G`.  Stated, not proved, as
`GGT.DGOTheorem68` in `GGT/WPDAcylindricalHyperbolicity.lean`, against
`GGT.AH3Data` (an isometric action on a `δ`-hyperbolic space, a loxodromic
element, and `IsWPDAt`) and `¬ GGT.IsVirtuallyCyclic G` -- and that last
hypothesis is free here: `not_isVirtuallyCyclic_of_suitable` below derives it
from clause (2) of Hull's Definition 1.4.

Note what it does *not* give: its conclusion is `GGT.IsHypEmbedded G E`, i.e.
`E ↪_h (G, X)` for *some* relative generating set `X`, not for Hull's `A`.
Upgrading an arbitrary `X` to one containing `A`, with the action on the
resulting coned-off graph still acylindrical, is exactly the content of (2),
and it is the step that makes the alphabet of the quotient in (8) comparable
with the alphabet of `G`.  Over a torsion-free ambient group `E(g) = ⟨g⟩`,
which is why the torsion clauses of (6) are free in the lane this repository
runs; that identification is owned by the `ggt-elementary` lane.

**(4) The cone-off is hyperbolic, and the rotating family.**  Coning `Γ(G,A)`
off along the cosets of `H = E(g)` leaves it hyperbolic (DGO §5, the
hyperbolic cone-off); the conjugates of a subgroup `⟨⟨W⟩⟩ ≤ H` then form a
separated *very rotating family* on the cone-off, with the cosets `aH` as
apices and `a ⟨⟨W⟩⟩ a⁻¹` as rotation subgroups.  Definitions and the
elementary displacement estimates: `GGT/HullSCRotatingFamily.lean`.

**(5) DGO, Theorem 5.3.**  For a separated very rotating family the quotient by
`K = ⟨⟨Rot c⟩⟩` satisfies: `K` is a free product of conjugates of the rotation
subgroups; every element of `K` is conjugate into some `Rot c` or is
loxodromic; and every finite subgroup of the quotient lifts.  Stated as
`HullSC.DGOQuotientStatement`, cited, not proved.

**(6) Hull, Theorem 5.1.**  The small cancellation quotient: for
`{Hλ} ↪_h (G,X)` and a word `W` satisfying Hull's condition `C(ε, μ, ρ)` the
quotient `G → G/⟨⟨W⟩⟩` is injective on a ball of prescribed radius, carries the
`Hλ` to hyperbolically embedded subgroups, is acylindrically hyperbolic, and
lifts finite order.  This is (5) applied to the family of (4).

**(7) Hull, §6 with Corollary 5.7 and Lemma 5.8.**  Given a suitable `N` and a
target `t ∈ G`, choose two independent loxodromics `h₁, h₂ ∈ N`, and a word
`W = t⁻¹ u` with `u = h₁^{n₁} h₂^{n₂} ⋯ ∈ N` satisfying `C(ε, μ, ρ)`.  This is
the whole trick: the relator is a single element, `ker q = ⟨⟨t⁻¹u⟩⟩` is
normally generated by one element, and `q(t) = q(u) ∈ q(N)` holds by
construction.  Over a torsion-free `G` the choice of `h₁, h₂` is
`Manuscript.NonMF.HullInputsProved.exists_pair_suitable_of_torsionFree`, which
is proved.

**(8) Hull, Theorem 7.1 for `m = 1`.**  (6) with the relator of (7):
`HullSC.HullOneStepStatement`.  **This is the single citation of this
module.**  It carries three clauses beyond the manuscript's `thm:hull`, all of
them properties of Hull's construction rather than additions to it: the
alphabet of the quotient is the image of the alphabet of `G`, the image of a
prescribed suitable subgroup stays suitable, and the kernel is the normal
closure of one element.

**(9) Hull, Theorem 7.1, general `m`.**  Induction on `m`, applying (8) to
`t₁`, then to the image of `t₂` in the quotient, and so on.  Proved here:
`hullTower_of_oneStep`.  The induction is possible only because of the
alphabet clause: the balls of `Γ(G,A)` are infinite when `A` is, and Hull's `A`
is infinite in general, so the image of the `R`-ball of `Γ(G,A)` is not a
finite set to be swallowed by a larger ball -- it has to be shown to sit inside
the `R`-ball of `Γ(Q,A')` at the *same* radius, which is
`HullSC.image_cayleyBall_subset`.

**(10) The ball form, and the finite-set form.**  `hullBallFormNG_of_tower`
gives `HullCorrectedInputs.HullBallFormStatementNG`;
`HullCorrectedInputs.smallCancellation_of_ballFormNG`, already in the
repository, turns it into the finite-set form the manuscript records.  Nothing
of either is restated here.

## What this module contributes

The reduction (8) ⟹ (9) ⟹ (10), with the kernel bookkeeping.  Everything below
(8) is definitions and elementary lemmas, in the two companion modules; (8)
itself is the citation, and it is the only one.  The arithmetic of the kernel
-- that `m` steps, each killing one element, leave a kernel normally generated
by `m` elements -- is `ker_comp_eq` and is proved, so the
manuscript's separate remark on Hull's construction is a citation only at
`m = 1`.

Two hypotheses the chain would otherwise have to carry are also discharged
here rather than assumed: `not_isVirtuallyCyclic_of_suitable` derives the
hypothesis of `GGT.DGOTheorem68` from Hull's Definition 1.4, and
`HullSC.isHypEmbeddedOf_coneOff` identifies the cone-off as the witness for
`H ↪_h (G,A)`, so step (3) reduces to hyperbolicity and local finiteness of
one explicit relative Cayley graph.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace HullSC

open GroupApproximation.HullGeometry
open GroupApproximation.Manuscript.NonMF.TorsionFree

universe u v w

/-! ## The kernel of a composite -/

/-- **The kernel of a composite is normally generated by the union of a
generating set of the first kernel with lifts of a generating set of the
second.**

`r` is the union: it contains a normal generating set `s` of `ker φ`, and for
every normal generator `v` of `ker ψ` a `φ`-preimage of `v`.  Both inclusions
are elementary, but the interesting one uses surjectivity twice: to lift the
generators of `ker ψ`, and to know that the image of a normal subgroup is
normal. -/
theorem ker_comp_eq {G : Type u} {Q : Type v} {P : Type w} [Group G] [Group Q]
    [Group P] (φ : G →* Q) (hφ : Function.Surjective φ) (ψ : Q →* P)
    (s : Set G) (uu : Set Q) (r : Set G)
    (hker₁ : φ.ker = Subgroup.normalClosure s)
    (hker₂ : ψ.ker = Subgroup.normalClosure uu)
    (hsr : s ⊆ r) (hlift : ∀ v ∈ uu, ∃ y ∈ r, φ y = v)
    (hkill : ∀ y ∈ r, ψ (φ y) = 1) :
    (ψ.comp φ).ker = Subgroup.normalClosure r := by
  have hrn : (Subgroup.normalClosure r).Normal := inferInstance
  haveI : ((Subgroup.normalClosure r).map φ).Normal := by
    refine ⟨?_⟩
    intro n hn g
    rw [Subgroup.mem_map] at hn
    obtain ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩ := hn
    obtain ⟨z, rfl⟩ := hφ g
    refine Subgroup.mem_map.mpr ⟨z * x * z⁻¹, hrn.conj_mem x hx z, ?_⟩
    rw [map_mul, map_mul, map_inv]
  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_
  · intro x hx
    rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, MonoidHom.comp_apply] at hx
    have hφx : φ x ∈ Subgroup.normalClosure uu := by
      rw [← hker₂, MonoidHom.mem_ker]
      exact hx
    have hle : Subgroup.normalClosure uu ≤ (Subgroup.normalClosure r).map φ := by
      refine Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal ?_
      intro v hv
      obtain ⟨y, hy, hyv⟩ := hlift v hv
      exact SetLike.mem_coe.mpr
        (Subgroup.mem_map.mpr ⟨y, Subgroup.subset_normalClosure hy, hyv⟩)
    obtain ⟨y, hy, hyx⟩ := Subgroup.mem_map.mp (hle hφx)
    have hmem : y⁻¹ * x ∈ φ.ker := by
      rw [MonoidHom.mem_ker, map_mul, map_inv, hyx]
      simp
    rw [hker₁] at hmem
    have hins : y⁻¹ * x ∈ Subgroup.normalClosure r :=
      Subgroup.normalClosure_mono hsr hmem
    have hxmem : y * (y⁻¹ * x) ∈ Subgroup.normalClosure r := mul_mem hy hins
    rwa [mul_inv_cancel_left] at hxmem
  · refine Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal ?_
    intro y hy
    exact SetLike.mem_coe.mpr (MonoidHom.mem_ker.mpr (hkill y hy))

/-! ## The output of Hull's theorem, with the data his construction carries -/

/-- **One quotient in Hull's tower.**

The fields beyond the manuscript's `thm:hull` are the three properties of
Hull's construction that the induction on `m` consumes:

* `alphabet_image` -- the alphabet of the quotient contains the image of the
  alphabet of `G`.  Hull's Theorem 5.1 produces the quotient's hyperbolically
  embedded structure over the *image* of the relative generating set, so this
  is a reading of his conclusion, not an addition to it.  Without it there is
  no control of the injectivity radius of a composite: see
  `HullSC.image_cayleyBall_subset`.
* `suitable_map`, `suitable_map_family` -- the image of the suitable subgroup
  the theorem is applied to, and of any prescribed finite family of suitable
  subgroups, stays suitable.  Hull's own induction needs the first of these,
  since the second step is applied to the image of `N`; the second is what
  Corollary 7.4 needs, since there the subgroup that must survive the first
  quotient is not the one the first quotient was taken along. -/
structure HullStep {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G)
    (N : Subgroup G) {k : ℕ} (S : Fin k → Subgroup G) (R : ℕ) where
  /-- The quotient group. -/
  Q : Type u
  /-- Its group structure. -/
  [group : Group Q]
  /-- The quotient map. -/
  q : G →* Q
  /-- The quotient map is onto. -/
  surjective : Function.Surjective q
  /-- Hull's alphabet for the quotient. -/
  hullSet : HullGeneratingSet Q
  /-- It contains the image of Hull's alphabet for `G`. -/
  alphabet_image : ∀ a ∈ A.alphabet.carrier, q a ∈ hullSet.alphabet.carrier
  /-- The quotient map is injective on the `R`-ball of `Γ(G,A)`. -/
  injOn : Set.InjOn q (cayleyBall A.alphabet R)
  /-- The image of `N` is suitable in the quotient. -/
  suitable_map : Suitable hullSet.alphabet (N.map q)
  /-- The image of each prescribed suitable subgroup is suitable. -/
  suitable_map_family : ∀ j : Fin k, Suitable hullSet.alphabet ((S j).map q)
  /-- Every element of finite order in the quotient is the image of an element
  of the same order. -/
  finiteOrder_lift :
    ∀ y : Q, IsOfFinOrder y → ∃ g : G, q g = y ∧ orderOf g = orderOf y

namespace HullStep

instance instGroup {G : Type u} [Group G] {A : HullGeneratingSet G}
    {N : Subgroup G} {k : ℕ} {S : Fin k → Subgroup G} {R : ℕ}
    (s : HullStep A N S R) : Group s.Q := s.group

/-- **Two steps compose to a step.**  The alphabet clause is what makes the
injectivity radius of the composite the radius of both factors, through
`HullSC.injOn_comp_of_image_subset`; the two suitability clauses compose
because `(N.map q₁).map q₂ = N.map (q₂ ∘ q₁)`. -/
def comp {G : Type u} [Group G] {A : HullGeneratingSet G} {N : Subgroup G}
    {k : ℕ} {S : Fin k → Subgroup G} {R : ℕ} (s₁ : HullStep A N S R)
    (s₂ : HullStep s₁.hullSet (N.map s₁.q) (fun j => (S j).map s₁.q) R) :
    HullStep A N S R where
  Q := s₂.Q
  group := s₂.group
  q := s₂.q.comp s₁.q
  surjective := by
    intro y
    obtain ⟨z, hz⟩ := s₂.surjective y
    obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := s₁.surjective z
    exact ⟨x, by rw [MonoidHom.comp_apply, hx, hz]⟩
  hullSet := s₂.hullSet
  alphabet_image := by
    intro a ha
    exact s₂.alphabet_image _ (s₁.alphabet_image a ha)
  injOn :=
    injOn_comp_of_image_subset A.alphabet s₁.hullSet.alphabet s₁.q s₂.q
      s₁.alphabet_image R s₁.injOn s₂.injOn
  suitable_map := by
    have h := s₂.suitable_map
    rwa [Subgroup.map_map] at h
  suitable_map_family := by
    intro j
    have h := s₂.suitable_map_family j
    rwa [Subgroup.map_map] at h
  finiteOrder_lift := by
    intro y hy
    obtain ⟨z, hz, hzord⟩ := s₂.finiteOrder_lift y hy
    have hzfin : IsOfFinOrder z := by
      by_contra hcon
      have h0 : orderOf z = 0 := orderOf_eq_zero_iff.mpr hcon
      rw [h0] at hzord
      exact orderOf_eq_zero_iff.mp hzord.symm hy
    obtain ⟨x, hx, hxord⟩ := s₁.finiteOrder_lift z hzfin
    refine ⟨x, ?_, ?_⟩
    · rw [MonoidHom.comp_apply, hx, hz]
    · rw [hxord, hzord]

theorem comp_q {G : Type u} [Group G] {A : HullGeneratingSet G} {N : Subgroup G}
    {k : ℕ} {S : Fin k → Subgroup G} {R : ℕ} (s₁ : HullStep A N S R)
    (s₂ : HullStep s₁.hullSet (N.map s₁.q) (fun j => (S j).map s₁.q) R) :
    (s₁.comp s₂).q = s₂.q.comp s₁.q := rfl

end HullStep

/-- **Hull's Theorem 7.1, in the form the induction on `m` produces it.**  A
`HullStep` together with the two clauses that mention the targets `t₁, …, t_m`:
each `q(tᵢ)` lies in `q(N)`, and `ker q` is normally generated by `m`
elements. -/
structure HullTower {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G)
    (N : Subgroup G) {k : ℕ} (S : Fin k → Subgroup G) {m : ℕ} (t : Fin m → G)
    (R : ℕ) where
  /-- The quotient, with the data of Hull's construction. -/
  step : HullStep A N S R
  /-- `q(tᵢ) ∈ q(N)` for every `i`. -/
  mem_map : ∀ i : Fin m, step.q (t i) ∈ N.map step.q
  /-- `ker q` is the normal closure of at most `m` elements. -/
  kerNormallyGenerated : ∃ T : Finset G, T.card ≤ m ∧
    step.q.ker = Subgroup.normalClosure (T : Set G)

/-- A `HullGeneratingSet` witnesses acylindrical hyperbolicity: the class is
that data, existentially quantified. -/
theorem isAcylindricallyHyperbolic_of_hullGeneratingSet {G : Type u} [Group G]
    (A : HullGeneratingSet G) : IsAcylindricallyHyperbolic G :=
  ⟨⟨A.alphabet, A.delta, A.hyperbolic, A.acylindrical, A.nonElementary⟩⟩

/-! ## Suitability discharges the hypothesis of DGO's Theorem 6.8 -/

/-- **Two independent loxodromics have no common nonzero power.**

If `g ^ m = h ^ k` with `k ≠ 0`, then `g ^ (m·N) = h ^ (k·N)` for every `N`, so
the two power orbits meet; at a point where they meet the Gromov product is the
distance from the basepoint, and loxodromy of `h ^ k` makes that unbounded,
contradicting the bound independence supplies.  With `k = 0` the equation reads
`g ^ m = 1`, and a loxodromic element has infinite order.

This is the argument of `HullGeometry.notMem_zpowers_of_independent`, run for
powers on both sides rather than for `h` itself. -/
theorem eq_zero_of_common_zpow_of_independent {G : Type u} [Group G]
    {X : Type v} [PseudoMetricSpace X] [MulAction G X]
    (hiso : IsIsometricAction G X) {g h : G} {x : X}
    (hg : IsLoxodromic g x) (hh : IsLoxodromic h x) (hind : Independent g h x)
    {m k : ℤ} (hmk : g ^ m = h ^ k) : m = 0 ∧ k = 0 := by
  obtain ⟨C, hC⟩ := hind
  have hk0 : k = 0 := by
    by_contra hk
    have hlox : IsLoxodromic (h ^ k) x := isLoxodromic_zpow hiso hh hk
    obtain ⟨N, hN⟩ :=
      Filter.eventually_atTop.mp (hlox.isEscaping.eventually_ge_atTop (C + 1))
    have hkey := hC (m * (N : ℤ)) (k * (N : ℤ))
    have hgpow : g ^ (m * (N : ℤ)) = h ^ (k * (N : ℤ)) := by
      rw [zpow_mul, zpow_mul, hmk]
    rw [hgpow, gromovProduct_self, dist_comm] at hkey
    have hfar := hN N le_rfl
    have hpow : (h ^ k) ^ N = h ^ (k * (N : ℤ)) := by
      rw [zpow_mul, zpow_natCast]
    rw [hpow] at hfar
    linarith
  refine ⟨?_, hk0⟩
  have hginj : Function.Injective (fun n : ℤ => g ^ n) :=
    injective_zpow_iff_not_isOfFinOrder.mpr
      (not_isOfFinOrder_of_isLoxodromic hg)
  have hone : g ^ m = g ^ (0 : ℤ) := by
    rw [hmk, hk0, zpow_zero, zpow_zero]
  exact hginj hone

/-- **A group with a non-elementary subgroup is not virtually cyclic.** -/
theorem not_isVirtuallyCyclic_of_actsNonElementarily {G : Type u} [Group G]
    {X : Type v} [PseudoMetricSpace X] [MulAction G X]
    (hiso : IsIsometricAction G X) {S : Subgroup G} {x : X}
    (hne : ActsNonElementarily S x) : ¬ GGT.IsVirtuallyCyclic G := by
  obtain ⟨g, -, h, -, hg, hh, hind⟩ := hne
  exact GGT.not_isVirtuallyCyclic_of_no_common_power
    (fun m k hmk => eq_zero_of_common_zpow_of_independent hiso hg hh hind hmk)

/-- **The hypothesis of `GGT.DGOTheorem68` is free in Hull's setting.**  That
citation asks for `¬ IsVirtuallyCyclic G` before it produces the hyperbolically
embedded elementary closure `E(g)`; clause (2) of Hull's Definition 1.4 --
`N` contains two independent loxodromics -- already gives it.  So step (3) of
the plan above costs the chain nothing beyond the citation itself. -/
theorem not_isVirtuallyCyclic_of_suitable {G : Type u} [Group G]
    {A : Alphabet G} {N : Subgroup G} (h : Suitable A N) :
    ¬ GGT.IsVirtuallyCyclic G :=
  not_isVirtuallyCyclic_of_actsNonElementarily (isIsometricAction_cayley A)
    h.actsNonElementarily

/-! ## The citation, and the induction -/

/-- **Hull, Theorem 7.1, for a single target element.**  This is the only
statement of this development that is cited rather than proved.

> Let `G` be acylindrically hyperbolic with Hull's alphabet `A`, let `N ≤ G` be
> suitable, let `S₁, …, S_k ≤ G` be suitable, let `t ∈ G` and let `R` be a
> radius.  Then there is a surjection `q : G ↠ Q` onto an acylindrically
> hyperbolic group with Hull alphabet containing `q(A)`, injective on the
> `R`-ball of `Γ(G,A)`, with `q(N)` and every `q(Sⱼ)` suitable, with `q(t) ∈
> q(N)`, lifting finite order, and with `ker q` the normal closure of a single
> element.

The proof is Hull's: choose two independent loxodromics `h₁, h₂` in `N`, form
the hyperbolically embedded elementary closure `E(h₁)`, choose
`u = h₁^{n₁}h₂^{n₂}⋯ ∈ N` making `W = t⁻¹u` satisfy his small cancellation
condition `C(ε,μ,ρ)`, and quotient by `⟨⟨W⟩⟩` using his Theorem 5.1, which is
DGO's Theorem 5.3 for the very rotating family of conjugates of `⟨⟨W⟩⟩` on the
cone-off of `Γ(G,A)` along the cosets of `E(h₁)`.  Then `q(t) = q(u) ∈ q(N)`
and `ker q = ⟨⟨W⟩⟩` are true by construction, and the remaining clauses are
the conclusions of Theorem 5.1. -/
def HullOneStepStatement : Prop :=
  ∀ {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G) {N : Subgroup G},
    Suitable A.alphabet N → ∀ {k : ℕ} {S : Fin k → Subgroup G},
      (∀ j : Fin k, Suitable A.alphabet (S j)) → ∀ (t : G) (R : ℕ),
        Nonempty (HullTower A N S (fun _ : Fin 1 => t) R)

/-- **Hull, Theorem 7.1 for every `m`.** -/
def HullTowerStatement : Prop :=
  ∀ {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G) {N : Subgroup G},
    Suitable A.alphabet N → ∀ {k : ℕ} {S : Fin k → Subgroup G},
      (∀ j : Fin k, Suitable A.alphabet (S j)) → ∀ {m : ℕ} (t : Fin m → G)
        (R : ℕ), Nonempty (HullTower A N S t R)

/-- **The induction on `m`.**  Hull's Theorem 7.1 for `m` targets follows from
his theorem for one target, applied `m` times along the tower.

At the base the quotient is the identity: nothing is killed, so the kernel is
normally generated by the empty set.  At the inductive step the first target is
absorbed by one application of the citation, and the remaining `m` targets are
absorbed by the inductive hypothesis applied to the *quotient* -- which is
legitimate because the quotient carries a Hull alphabet in which the image of
`N` is again suitable, and because the image of the `R`-ball of `Γ(G,A)` sits
in the `R`-ball of the quotient's Cayley graph at the same radius.  The kernels
compose by `ker_comp_eq`, one new normal generator per step. -/
theorem hullTower_of_oneStep (h : HullOneStepStatement.{u}) :
    ∀ (m : ℕ) {G : Type u} [Group G] (A : HullGeneratingSet G)
      {N : Subgroup G}, Suitable A.alphabet N → ∀ {k : ℕ}
        {S : Fin k → Subgroup G}, (∀ j : Fin k, Suitable A.alphabet (S j)) →
          ∀ (t : Fin m → G) (R : ℕ), Nonempty (HullTower A N S t R) := by
  intro m
  induction m with
  | zero =>
      intro G _ A N hN k S hS t R
      have hbot : Subgroup.normalClosure ((∅ : Finset G) : Set G) = ⊥ := by
        rw [Finset.coe_empty]
        exact le_antisymm
          (Subgroup.normalClosure_le_normal (Set.empty_subset _)) bot_le
      have hker : (MonoidHom.id G).ker = ⊥ := by
        ext x
        simp [MonoidHom.mem_ker]
      refine ⟨{
        step :=
          { Q := G
            group := inferInstance
            q := MonoidHom.id G
            surjective := fun y => ⟨y, rfl⟩
            hullSet := A
            alphabet_image := fun a ha => ha
            injOn := fun x _ y _ hxy => hxy
            suitable_map := by rw [Subgroup.map_id]; exact hN
            suitable_map_family := by
              intro j
              rw [Subgroup.map_id]
              exact hS j
            finiteOrder_lift := fun y _ => ⟨y, rfl, rfl⟩ }
        mem_map := fun i => Fin.elim0 i
        kerNormallyGenerated := by
          refine ⟨∅, by simp, ?_⟩
          show (MonoidHom.id G).ker
            = Subgroup.normalClosure ((∅ : Finset G) : Set G)
          rw [hker, hbot] }⟩
  | succ m ih =>
      intro G _ A N hN k S hS t R
      classical
      obtain ⟨s₁⟩ := h A hN hS (t 0) R
      obtain ⟨s₂⟩ := ih (N := N.map s₁.step.q)
        (S := fun j => (S j).map s₁.step.q) s₁.step.hullSet
        s₁.step.suitable_map s₁.step.suitable_map_family
        (fun i : Fin m => s₁.step.q (t i.succ)) R
      -- the composite quotient
      have hmapmap : (N.map s₁.step.q).map s₂.step.q
          = N.map (s₂.step.q.comp s₁.step.q) := by
        rw [Subgroup.map_map]
      have hmem : ∀ i : Fin (m + 1),
          (s₂.step.q.comp s₁.step.q) (t i) ∈ N.map (s₂.step.q.comp s₁.step.q) := by
        intro i
        refine Fin.cases ?_ ?_ i
        · have h0 : s₂.step.q (s₁.step.q (t 0))
              ∈ (N.map s₁.step.q).map s₂.step.q :=
            Subgroup.mem_map_of_mem _ (s₁.mem_map 0)
          rw [hmapmap] at h0
          exact h0
        · intro j
          have hj := s₂.mem_map j
          rw [hmapmap] at hj
          exact hj
      -- the kernel of the composite
      have hkercomp : ∃ T : Finset G, T.card ≤ m + 1 ∧
          (s₂.step.q.comp s₁.step.q).ker = Subgroup.normalClosure (T : Set G) := by
        obtain ⟨T₁, hT₁card, hT₁ker⟩ := s₁.kerNormallyGenerated
        obtain ⟨T₂, hT₂card, hT₂ker⟩ := s₂.kerNormallyGenerated
        obtain ⟨σ, hσ⟩ : ∃ σ : s₁.step.Q → G, ∀ v, s₁.step.q (σ v) = v := by
          choose σ hσ using s₁.step.surjective
          exact ⟨σ, hσ⟩
        refine ⟨T₁ ∪ T₂.image σ, ?_, ?_⟩
        · refine le_trans (Finset.card_union_le _ _) ?_
          have himg : (T₂.image σ).card ≤ m :=
            le_trans Finset.card_image_le hT₂card
          omega
        · refine ker_comp_eq s₁.step.q s₁.step.surjective s₂.step.q
            (T₁ : Set G) (T₂ : Set s₁.step.Q) ((T₁ ∪ T₂.image σ : Finset G) : Set G)
            hT₁ker hT₂ker ?_ ?_ ?_
          · intro x hx
            exact Finset.mem_coe.mpr
              (Finset.mem_union_left _ (Finset.mem_coe.mp hx))
          · intro v hv
            refine ⟨σ v, ?_, hσ v⟩
            exact Finset.mem_coe.mpr (Finset.mem_union_right _
              (Finset.mem_image_of_mem _ (Finset.mem_coe.mp hv)))
          · intro y hy
            rcases Finset.mem_union.mp (Finset.mem_coe.mp hy) with hy₁ | hy₂
            · have hy1 : s₁.step.q y = 1 := by
                rw [← MonoidHom.mem_ker, hT₁ker]
                exact Subgroup.subset_normalClosure (Finset.mem_coe.mpr hy₁)
              rw [hy1, map_one]
            · obtain ⟨v, hv, hvy⟩ := Finset.mem_image.mp hy₂
              have hqy : s₁.step.q y = v := by rw [← hvy]; exact hσ v
              rw [hqy, ← MonoidHom.mem_ker, hT₂ker]
              exact Subgroup.subset_normalClosure (Finset.mem_coe.mpr hv)
      exact ⟨{ step := s₁.step.comp s₂.step
               mem_map := hmem
               kerNormallyGenerated := hkercomp }⟩

/-- **Hull's Theorem 7.1 for every `m`, from the one-relator case.** -/
theorem hullTowerStatement_of_oneStep (h : HullOneStepStatement.{u}) :
    HullTowerStatement.{u} := by
  intro G _ A N hN k S hS m t R
  exact hullTower_of_oneStep h m A hN hS t R

/-! ## The published ball form -/

/-- **Hull's Theorem 7.1 in its published injectivity-radius form**, with the
manuscript's kernel datum, from the tower.

This is `Manuscript.NonMF.HullCorrectedInputs.HullBallFormStatementNG`, so
`HullCorrectedInputs.smallCancellation_of_ballFormNG` turns it into the
finite-set form `HullInputsCorrected.smallCancellation` records, and
`Saturation.saturation` follows.  Neither of those two steps is restated
here. -/
theorem hullBallFormNG_of_tower (h : HullTowerStatement.{u}) :
    Manuscript.NonMF.HullCorrectedInputs.HullBallFormStatementNG.{u} := by
  intro G _ _ A N hN m t R
  obtain ⟨D⟩ := h (k := 0) (S := fun j : Fin 0 => Fin.elim0 j) A hN
    (fun j : Fin 0 => Fin.elim0 j) t R
  obtain ⟨T, hTcard, hTker⟩ := D.kerNormallyGenerated
  exact ⟨{ quotient :=
             { Q := D.step.Q
               group := D.step.group
               q := D.step.q
               surjective := D.step.surjective
               acylindricallyHyperbolic :=
                 isAcylindricallyHyperbolic_of_hullGeneratingSet D.step.hullSet
               injOn := D.step.injOn
               mem_map := D.mem_map
               finiteOrder_lift := D.step.finiteOrder_lift }
           kerNormallyGenerated := ⟨T, hTcard, hTker⟩ }⟩

/-- **Hull's Theorem 7.1, from its one-relator case.**  The composite of the
two reductions: this is what closing `HullOneStepStatement` closes. -/
theorem hullBallFormNG_of_oneStep (h : HullOneStepStatement.{u}) :
    Manuscript.NonMF.HullCorrectedInputs.HullBallFormStatementNG.{u} :=
  hullBallFormNG_of_tower (hullTowerStatement_of_oneStep h)

